¶
'''assassin†''' (ạ-sas′in), ''v. t.'' [< F. ''assassiner'', assassinate, worry, vex, = It. ''assassinare'', assassinate, < ML. ''assassinare''; from the noun.] To murder; assassinate.
{{smaller block|With him that ''assassines'' his parents.
{{right|''Stillingfleet'', Sermons, p. 502.}}}}

'''assassinacy†''' (ạ-sas′i-nạ̄-si), ''n.'' [< ''assassina(te)'' + ''-cy''.] The act of assassinating. ''Hammond.''

'''assassinant†''' (ạ-sas′i-nant), ''n.'' [< F. ''assassinant'', ppr. of ''assassiner'': see ''assassin'', ''v.''] An assassin.

'''assassinate''' (ạ-sas′i-nāt), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''assassinated'', ppr. ''assassinating''. [< ML. ''assassinatus'', pp. of ''assassinare'': see ''assassin'', ''v.''] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To kill or attempt to kill by surprise or secret assault; murder by sudden or treacherous violence.
{{smaller block|Help, neighbours, my house is broken open, . . . and I am ravished and like to be ''assassinated''. {{right|''Dryden.''}}}}
'''2†.''' To assault; maltreat.
{{smaller block|<poem>Such usage as your honourable lords
Afford me, ''assassinated'' and betray’d.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', S. A., l. 1109.}}}}
'''3.''' Figuratively, to blight or destroy treacherously; overthrow by foul or unfair means: as, to ''assassinate'' a person’s character or reputation. ='''Syn. 1.''' {{smaller|''Slay'', ''Murder'', etc. See ''kill''.}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To commit murder by assassination.
{{smaller block|Where now no thieves ''assassinate''.
{{right|''Sandys'', Paraphrase of Judges, v.}}}}
